---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro'
import Gloss from '../components/Gloss.astro'
import AssertionCard from '../components/AssertionCard.astro'
import { assertion, node, nodes } from '../lib/feeds'

const raid = assertion('the-night-the-jail-was-taken')

const bank = assertion('the-man-in-the-cell-was-charged-with-an-allen-county-bank')

const railTown = assertion('lima-was-a-railroad-town-before-an-oil-town')

const declared = assertion('ten-declarations-eight-incidents')

// The spine is read out of the graph, never typed here. A page that lists the county's dated
// record by hand would keep saying "1885" long after a phase had added something later, and the
// gap this page is largely about would be the first thing to go stale.
const year = (s: string | undefined) => (s ? Number(s.slice(0, 4)) : Number.NaN)

const events = nodes
  .filter((n) => n.class === 'event' && !Number.isNaN(year(n.properties.occurred)))
  .map((n) => ({ node: n, at: year(n.properties.occurred), on: n.properties.occurred }))
  .toSorted((a, b) => a.at - b.at)

const periods = nodes
  .filter((n) => n.class === 'period' && !Number.isNaN(year(n.properties.began)))
  .map((n) => ({ node: n, from: year(n.properties.began), to: year(n.properties.ended) }))
  .toSorted((a, b) => a.from - b.from)

// The longest run of years with no dated event in it, computed from the same list the table
// renders. Written as a pair so the prose below can name both ends without repeating itself.
const gaps = events.slice(1).map((e, i) => ({ from: events[i].at, to: e.at }))

const widest = gaps.reduce((a, b) => (b.to - b.from > a.to - a.from ? b : a), gaps[0])

const last = events[events.length - 1].at

const since = new Date().getUTCFullYear() - last
---

<Base
  title="History"
  lede="Everything this corpus can date, in order — and the stretches between. The gaps are the subject of this page as much as the entries are."
>
  <div class="reading">
  <section>
    <h2>The county's dated record, and the hole in the middle of it</h2>
    <p>
      A civic reference should be able to say when things happened. This one can say it {
        events.length
      } times. The list below is not a history of Allen County; it is the subset of the county's history that carries a date drawn from a citable source, which is a much
      smaller thing and is shown here at its real size.
    </p>
    <table>
      <caption>Dated events in the corpus, earliest first</caption>
      <thead>
        <tr><th scope="col">Date</th><th scope="col">Event</th><th scope="col">Kind</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {
          events.map((e) => (
            <tr>
              <td>{e.on}</td>
              <td>{e.node.label}</td>
              <td>{e.node.properties.event_type ?? '—'}</td>
            </tr>
          ))
        }
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>
      The gaps are the point. The widest run with nothing dated in it is {widest.to - widest.from}
      years, from {widest.from} to {widest.to}; the longest of all is the {since} years since {last},
      which is where this corpus stops being able to name a day at all. That is not because nothing
      happened. It is because most of the work here has been counting rather than dating, and a count has no date on it — it has a year and a table.
    </p>
    <p>
      Between the oil strike and the raid the same pattern holds in reverse. The corpus can tell you
      that Lima's manufacturing workforce grew 82 per cent between 1899 and 1909 and that the county
      reached 56,580 people in 1910, and it cannot tell you the day either thing happened, because
      neither is the kind of thing that has one. What it has known across those years is what Allen
      County <em>was</em>. What happened in them is newer work.
    </p>
    <p>
      The most recent entry came from a land record rather than from a newspaper, and that is worth
      noticing about this list. A conveyance is dated to the day whether or not anybody reported it,
      and the county has abstracted its conveyances by piece of ground since the federal patents. A
      corpus that wants to know when things happened here has been reading the wrong kind of source.
    </p>
    <p>
      One entry on this list is here because the list is. The 1918 renaming of German Township was
      recorded as prose on a place and on a question — which is where an answer goes and not where
      a happening goes — so it was missing from every view built on the event class, and the
      stretch after 1885 read fifteen years emptier than the record actually was.
    </p>
    <Gloss>
      It had been established two phases earlier, with the day and the petition's own language.
      Publishing found it; reading had not.
    </Gloss>
    <p>
      The named spans do reach across it, because a period is a claim about a stretch rather than
      about a day:
    </p>
    <table>
      <caption>Named periods in the corpus</caption>
      <thead>
        <tr><th scope="col">Span</th><th scope="col">Period</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {
          periods.map((p) => (
            <tr>
              <td>{p.from}–{Number.isNaN(p.to) ? 'present' : p.to}</td>
              <td>{p.node.label}</td>
            </tr>
          ))
        }
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Why Lima is where it is</h2>
    <p>
      The county's industrial story is usually told as an oil story, and the oil is not where it starts. Two railroads chose to cross at Lima —
      one in 1854 and one four years later — and the canal towns fifteen miles west, which had
      the head start, did not get a crossing.
    </p>
    <AssertionCard assertion={railTown} />
    <p>
      The county paid $50,000 in stock for the first of them and later sold the stock at par, so
      the road cost it nothing. In 1872 Lima and Ottawa Township voted on $100,000 of bonds for
      two more lines: something over 700 votes were cast and eight of them were against. By 1883
      there were about 102 miles of main track in the county, and by 1906 two railroad shops in
      Lima employed more than five hundred men each.
    </p>
    <p>
      One date on the table above is not a date so much as a range. Three county histories name
      the same first railroad and give three different years — 1853, 1854 and 1856 — and none of
      them cites the record it read. The corpus takes 1854, because it is the nearest witness's
      year and because the same book that dates the opening latest also reports that the
      locomotive used to build the line arrived in 1854, by canal, as freight to Delphos. A
      construction engine delivered in 1854 cannot serve a line already open in 1853.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>The spine, and where it stopped</h2>
    <p>
      The table above is built from the corpus rather than typed here, and it ended in 1955 for a
      long time — sixteen events, none in the last seventy-one years, in a county with annual
      manufacturing employment since 1969, fifty-seven dated annexations, twenty-nine dated
      Register listings and the year every one of 364 bridges was built on the record beside it.
      The record was never missing. Every event here had come out of a book, and nothing that
      arrived as a dataset had ever been allowed to become one.
    </p>
    <AssertionCard assertion={declared} />
    <p>
      One API call moves the spine from 1955 to 2020. Ten federal disaster declarations name Allen
      County: tornadoes in 1965, a blizzard in 1978, then nothing for twenty-six years, then severe
      storms and flooding in 2004, 2005, 2007 and 2012, and COVID-19 in 2020. The rate looks like it
      rose tenfold and almost certainly did not — a programme that changed its coverage and its
      designation threshold is a likelier cause than north-west Ohio's weather changing by a factor
      of ten, and nothing in the file separates them.
    </p>
    <p>
      Two of the ten declarations brought Individual Assistance, which is money to households, in
      1965 and 2007. The other eight brought Public Assistance, which is money to governments for
      debris and public works. What a federal disaster has usually meant in this county is a bill to
      the county.
    </p>
    <p>
      Two of them are not what they look like. A county in north-west Ohio has a hurricane
      declaration — EM-3250-OH, September 2005, "Hurricane Katrina Evacuation", which reimburses a
      place for taking in people from somewhere else. And the longest incident in the county's
      federal record ran 1,207 days: COVID-19, from 20 January 2020 to 11 May 2023, against
      forty-one days for the longest of the other eight. The 2020 census was taken inside it.
    </p>
    <p>
      Counting the rows would have got this wrong. The June 2012 storm produced an emergency
      declaration on the 30th and a major disaster declaration seven weeks later for the same
      four-day incident; COVID-19 produced two in eighteen days. Ten declarations, eight incidents.
      And the incident dates before 2004 are not usable at all: the 1965 tornado and the 1978
      blizzard are each recorded as beginning and ending on the day the declaration was signed.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>The night the jail was taken</h2>
    <AssertionCard assertion={raid} />
    <p>
      Three men came into the Allen County jail at Lima after dark on 12 October 1933, demanded
      that the prisoner be handed over, shot Sheriff Jess L. Sarber when he fought them, locked his
      wife and his deputy in the cell they had just emptied, took every gun in the building and
      drove west. The prisoner was John Dillinger. Sarber died within the hour.
    </p>
    <AssertionCard assertion={bank} />
    <p>
      That is the part usually left out of the telling, and it is the part that makes this the
      county's event rather than a national one that happened to land here. Dillinger was in the
      Allen County jail because he was charged with robbing an Allen County bank — the Commercial
      Bank at Bluffton, in the north of the same county. The night started inside it.
    </p>
    <p>
      Harry Pierpont, Charles Makley and Russell Clark were tried at Lima the following spring,
      three trials of about a week each, with Ohio National Guardsmen holding the town — machine
      gun nests at the approaches to the jail, another at the end of the cell corridor, and nobody
      admitted without a written order from the sheriff, the prosecutor or the judge. Within
      twelve months and five days of the raid, Makley had been shot dead in an escape attempt,
      Pierpont had been electrocuted and Clark was serving life.
    </p>
  </section>

    <section class="onward">
      <h2>Where this goes on</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <a href="/read/the-fort-across-the-line">The fort across the line</a>
          <span class="why">The county's first settlement stands nine hundred and ninety feet outside it.</span>
        </li>
        <li>
          <a href="/read/who-the-county-is-named-for">Who the county is named for</a>
          <span class="why">A colonel of the War of 1812, and the earliest account cannot name him.</span>
        </li>
        <li>
          <a href="/read/one-person-in-ten">One person in ten</a>
          <span class="why">The county put 1,920 men into the Civil War and never added up its own dead.</span>
        </li>
        <li>
          <a href="/read/three-times-a-crowd-came-to-the-jail">Three times a crowd came to the jail</a>
          <span class="why">Sixty-one years, three crowds, and an office that passed from father to son.</span>
        </li>
        <li>
          <a href="/read/two-counts-of-one-war">Two counts of one war</a>
          <span class="why">The county counted its World War soldiers twice and got different answers.</span>
        </li>
        <li>
          <a href="/read/what-the-sky-did">What the sky did</a>
          <span class="why">A thirty-fold rise in severe weather reports that is mostly categories arriving.</span>
        </li>
        <li>
          <a href="/read#history">The reading room</a>
          <span class="why">All thirteen shorter pieces filed under this section.</span>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>
  </div>

  <script>
    import { renderCharts } from '../scripts/charts'
    renderCharts()
  </script>
</Base>
